      <section id="what">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink-900 mb-3">1. What is the product catalog?</h2>
        <p>
          The <strong>product catalog</strong> is the registry of all products that have been
          registered on COCOCE and are available for vendors to list for sale. It is the shared
          reference layer that sits between product families (the abstract product type) and
          listings (a vendor's actual offer with price and stock).
        </p>

        <div class="callout callout-info">
          <i data-lucide="lightbulb" class="w-5 h-5 callout-icon"></i>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
              <strong>In plain terms:</strong> a product catalog entry is a registered product.
              It defines <em>what</em> can be sold. A listing defines <em>who is selling it</em>,
              at what price, and with what stock.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>The catalog is what makes the marketplace work at scale:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Vendors search the catalog to find the product they want to sell;</li>
          <li>Buyers see the same product page regardless of which vendor they buy from;</li>
          <li>The platform can compare, recommend, and categorise products consistently;</li>
          <li>Duplicate entries are avoided — <em>Dell XPS 13</em> is one catalog entry, not one per vendor.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          A product catalog entry describes the product itself: title, description, brand, family,
          variants, and media. It does <strong>not</strong> contain price, stock, or vendor-specific
          information — those belong to listings.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="who" class="mt-10">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink-900 mb-3">2. Who can register products</h2>
        <p>
          Every vendor on COCOCE is allowed to request and register their own products in
          the catalog. This is intentional — the platform grows its catalog through vendor
          contribution, not through a central curation team.
        </p>

        <table class="policy-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th style="width:35%">Actor</th>
              <th style="width:65%">What they can do</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Vendor</strong></td>
              <td>Request and register new products. Register variants, descriptions, and media.
                Submit for verification. Cannot self-approve.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Lister</strong></td>
              <td>Browse the catalog. Create listings against any active product. Cannot
                register, edit, or remove catalog entries.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>System administrator</strong></td>
              <td>Verify, activate, disable, deny, or remove catalog entries. The only role
                with authority over the catalog's verified state.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="callout callout-info">
          <i data-lucide="info" class="w-5 h-5 callout-icon"></i>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
              <strong>Vendors register, admins verify.</strong> Registration and verification
              are separate steps by design. A vendor's submission is a <em>request</em> —
              it is not live until an admin has reviewed and activated it.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="lifecycle" class="mt-10">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink-900 mb-3">3. The lifecycle</h2>
        <p>
          Every product in the catalog moves through the same set of states. The lifecycle
          is intentionally linear — a vendor can withdraw a request, but cannot skip
          verification.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose rounded-xl border border-ink-200 overflow-hidden bg-white px-2.5">

          <div class="flow-step px-5">
            <div class="flow-num">1</div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink-900">Vendor registers the product</p>
              <p class="text-sm text-ink-500 mt-0.5">
                The vendor submits product information, variants, and images. The entry is
                created in the catalog with the status <code>requested</code>.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="flow-step is-passive px-5">
            <div class="flow-num">2</div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink-900">The product awaits review</p>
              <p class="text-sm text-ink-500 mt-0.5">
                The product is visible to the requesting vendor but not yet available for
                listings by other vendors or listers. It sits in the admin review queue.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="flow-step is-admin px-5">
            <div class="flow-num">3</div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink-900">Admin verifies the product</p>
              <p class="text-sm text-ink-500 mt-0.5">
                A system administrator reviews the entry against the
                <a href="/catalog-policy">Catalog Policy</a> — accuracy of information,
                completeness, naming conventions, and appropriateness.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="flow-step is-success px-5">
            <div class="flow-num">4</div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink-900">Product is activated</p>
              <p class="text-sm text-ink-500 mt-0.5">
                Once verified, the product is <strong>activated</strong>. It becomes visible
                to all vendors and listers on the platform, and can be listed for sale.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="flow-step px-5">
            <div class="flow-num">5</div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink-900">Vendors create listings</p>
              <p class="text-sm text-ink-500 mt-0.5">
                Any vendor can now create a store listing against the product — setting
                their own price, stock, and fulfilment terms. Listings reference the catalog
                entry but are otherwise independent.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="verification" class="mt-10">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink-900 mb-3">4. Verification &amp; activation</h2>
        <p>
          Verification is the gate between a vendor's request and the shared catalog. Only
          system administrators have the authority to verify and activate products.
        </p>

        <p>During verification, an admin checks:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Accurate information</strong> — the title, description, brand, family, and
            variants correctly describe a real product;</li>
          <li><strong>Completeness</strong> — at least one image, at least one variant, and all
            required fields filled;</li>
          <li><strong>Naming conventions</strong> — the entry follows the standards in the
            <a href="/catalog-policy">Catalog Policy</a>;
          </li>
          <li><strong>Non-duplication</strong> — no equivalent product already exists in the
            catalog;</li>
          <li><strong>Appropriateness</strong> — the product is permitted on the platform
            and suitable for the COCOCE audience.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="callout callout-warn">
          <i data-lucide="alert-triangle" class="w-5 h-5 callout-icon"></i>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
              <strong>Vendors cannot self-approve.</strong> Even a vendor who has previously
              registered dozens of products goes through the same review. The catalog is
              shared, and verification ensures every entry meets the same bar.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          A verified product is marked <code>active</code> and becomes immediately available
          for listing by any vendor on the platform.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="listing" class="mt-10">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink-900 mb-3">5. From catalog to listing</h2>
        <p>
          The catalog and listings are distinct layers. Understanding the split matters
          because they have different owners and different rules.
        </p>

        <table class="policy-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th style="width:25%"></th>
              <th style="width:37.5%">Product catalog</th>
              <th style="width:37.5%">Store listing</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Answers</strong></td>
              <td>What is this product?</td>
              <td>Who is selling it, and at what price?</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Shared by</strong></td>
              <td>All vendors</td>
              <td>One vendor per listing</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Contains</strong></td>
              <td>Title, description, brand, family, variants, media</td>
              <td>Price, stock, condition, shipping terms, vendor</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Created by</strong></td>
              <td>Any vendor — verified by admin</td>
              <td>Any vendor — no admin approval</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Editable by</strong></td>
              <td>Admin only, once activated</td>
              <td>The owning vendor, at any time</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="callout callout-info">
          <i data-lucide="package" class="w-5 h-5 callout-icon"></i>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
              <strong>One product, many listings.</strong> A single <em>Dell XPS 13</em>
              catalog entry can be listed by a dozen different vendors, each at their own
              price and with their own stock. Buyers see the same product page and can
              compare offers.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="removal" class="mt-10">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink-900 mb-3">6. Denial &amp; removal</h2>
        <p>
          Administrators are responsible for keeping the catalog accurate and trustworthy.
          When a product fails verification, or when an activated product is later found to
          be incorrect, admins take action.
        </p>

        <div class="callout callout-danger">
          <i data-lucide="shield-alert" class="w-5 h-5 callout-icon"></i>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
              <strong>Only system administrators can deny, disable, or remove a catalog
                product.</strong> Vendors cannot delete their own submissions once other
              vendors have begun listing against them.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>Admins take action when a product has:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Incorrect or misleading information;</li>
          <li>Duplicate entries (an equivalent product already exists);</li>
          <li>Naming that violates the Catalog Policy;</li>
          <li>Product images that infringe copyright or misrepresent the item;</li>
          <li>Content that is not permitted on the platform.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>Depending on the severity, admins can:</p>

        <table class="policy-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th style="width:25%">Action</th>
              <th style="width:75%">Effect</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Deny</strong></td>
              <td>Reject a new submission. The requesting vendor is notified with a reason
                and may correct and resubmit.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Disable</strong></td>
              <td>Hide an active product from new listings while keeping existing listings
                functional. Reversible.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Remove</strong></td>
              <td>Delete the product from the catalog. Reserved for clear errors and
                duplicates. Any listings referencing it are affected.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>
          Disable is preferred over remove. A disabled product can be re-enabled if the
          concern turns out to be unfounded; a removed product cannot be brought back.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="roles" class="mt-10">
